<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<autofill-service xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:settingsActivity="com.localkeep.app.MainActivity"
    android:supportsInlineSuggestions="true">

    <!--
        Compatibility mode, requested for the browsers that do not hand their page content to the
        autofill framework natively. Without a request here the platform never enables it for us,
        so a login typed into a web page in one of these browsers was offered nothing and captured
        nothing. The tag itself is the platform's, documented in the SDK's own attrs.xml
        (android-36/data/res/values/attrs.xml, declare-styleable
        "AutofillService_CompatibilityPackage"): "Use compatibility-package as a child tag of
        autofill-service ... to specify a package and an optional max version code for which to
        request compatibility mode."

        Two things this tag deliberately does NOT carry, because it cannot:

        1. The URL bar resource id. Those live in the device's own allowlist, not here. The
           framework reads them from the DeviceConfig property "compat_mode_allowed_packages"
           (AutofillFeatureFlags.DEVICE_CONFIG_AUTOFILL_COMPAT_MODE_ALLOWED_PACKAGES), whose format
           its javadoc gives as "p1[url_bar]:p2:p3[url_foo,url_bas]", and hands them to
           AutofillCompatState.addCompatibilityModeRequest in
           services/autofill/java/com/android/server/autofill/AutofillManagerService.java. The
           styleable above accepts "name" and "maxLongVersionCode" and nothing else, so an id
           written here would be silently ignored rather than applied.
        2. A max version code. Omitting it means every version
           (AutofillServiceInfo.parseCompatibilityPackages defaults maxVersionCode to
           Long.MAX_VALUE when the attribute is absent). The attribute exists to stop asking for
           compat mode once a browser supports autofill natively, and we have no verified version
           per browser at which that becomes true. Naming a made-up cut-off would disable filling
           in a browser that still needs it; asking for a version that no longer needs it costs
           nothing, because the same AutofillManagerService intersects this list with the device's
           allowlist and logs "Ignoring not allowed compat package" for the rest.

        Package ids verified against their Google Play listings on 2026-09-17
        (https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=<id>).
    -->
    <!-- Google Chrome -->
    <compatibility-package android:name="com.android.chrome" />
    <!-- Firefox Fast and Private Browser -->
    <compatibility-package android:name="org.mozilla.firefox" />
    <!-- Brave Private Web Browser -->
    <compatibility-package android:name="com.brave.browser" />
    <!-- Microsoft Edge -->
    <compatibility-package android:name="com.microsoft.emmx" />
    <!-- Samsung Internet Browser -->
    <compatibility-package android:name="com.sec.android.app.sbrowser" />
    <!-- Opera: Private Web Browser -->
    <compatibility-package android:name="com.opera.browser" />
    <!-- DuckDuckGo Private Browser -->
    <compatibility-package android:name="com.duckduckgo.mobile.android" />
</autofill-service>
